/// \defgroup grp_w1019_ini_units_name_keys Unit parsers, audio detail levels, post-load pass, name-key hashes, weapon and command-set lookups
/// \ingroup grp_ini
/// \brief The INI field parsers that turn times and rates into per-logic-frame
///        values, the AudioLOD block, the post-load pass over all
///        subsystems, the two name-key string hashes, weapon instancing and
///        command-set button lookup.
///
/// The functions in this group are small, but each sits where game data
/// meets the engine's fixed assumptions:
/// - Four INI field parsers (INI::parseDurationReal, INI::parseVelocityReal,
///   INI::parseAccelerationReal, INI::parseAngularVelocityReal) let data be
///   written in human units (milliseconds, per second, per second squared,
///   degrees per second) and store the value in logic-frame units. They are
///   where the logic rate enters the data.
/// - INI::parseAudioLODDefinition reads the AudioLOD block, the per-level
///   audio detail settings.
/// - SubsystemInterfaceList::postProcessLoadAll gives every subsystem a pass
///   after start-up data is loaded.
/// - calcHashForString and calcHashForLowercaseString pick the hash bucket
///   for NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey and NameKeyGenerator::nameToLowercaseKey.
/// - WeaponStore::allocateNewWeapon creates an object's Weapon from its
///   WeaponTemplate.
/// - CommandSet::getCommandButton returns one slot of a command set,
///   honouring run-time overrides recorded by the game logic.
///
/// The INI class page explains the field parser signature and how the reader
/// drives block and field parsers; \ref sub_ini is the subsystem page for the
/// load as a whole.
///
/// \par Units and the logic rate
/// The four unit parsers multiply the value read from the line by a factor
/// kept in writable global data, not by a compile-time constant:
/// - durations: frames per millisecond, initialised in retail to 0.005;
/// - velocities: seconds per logic frame, initialised to 0.2;
/// - accelerations: the square of seconds per logic frame, formed at run time
///   (0.04 at the initial value);
/// - angular velocities: seconds per logic frame times pi / 180.
///
/// \par
/// Both initial values describe the nominal 5 logic frames per second
/// (\ref page_frame_loop_clocks). Zero Hour writes the same parsers with the
/// GameCommon.h constants for its 30 logic frames per second
/// (LOGICFRAMES_PER_MSEC_REAL and SECONDS_PER_LOGICFRAME_REAL). The
/// duration factor and the seconds-per-frame factor are separate globals.
/// A separate routine in the executable takes two rates, a logic rate and a
/// client rate. It recomputes both factors from the logic rate, and related
/// client-rate values from the client rate. Before that it raises the client
/// rate to at least the logic rate and, when the whole-number ratio of the
/// client rate to the logic rate is above six, lowers it to six times the
/// logic rate. What calls the routine, and whether retail ever runs it, is
/// not established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Write durations in milliseconds, speeds in distance per second,
///   accelerations in distance per second squared and turn rates in degrees
///   per second. The parsers read the value with INI::scanReal, so the
///   \#ADD, \#MULTIPLY, \#SUBTRACT and \#DIVIDE math expressions described under
///   \ref sub_ini_pieces apply here too; see that page for what is and is
///   not established about macro expansion in BFME 2.
/// - No known INI field sets the factors or changes the units, and none of
///   the four parsers checks the range of the value: zero and negative
///   values are stored as converted.
/// - Many of these fields belong to logic-side definitions, such as weapon
///   speeds and turret turn rates. For those, a changed factor, like changed
///   data, desynchronises play with unmodified clients and changes how
///   recorded games play back.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The conversion happens once, when the field is read. Templates hold
///   per-frame values, so changing the logic rate after data has loaded
///   leaves every parsed value at the old rate; a different rate has to be in
///   place before the INI load. \ref page_frame_loop_modding and
///   \ref page_remastering_assumptions list the other places that assume
///   the nominal rates, including literal sixes that the factors do not
///   control.
/// - INI::load, INI::loadFile and the loader for a single named block each
///   call setFPMode before they parse anything. That puts the x87 unit into
///   24-bit (single) precision with round-to-nearest (\ref sub_ini_remaster),
///   and the four parsers do their multiplications with x87 instructions. To
///   reproduce retail values, compute in single precision with
///   round-to-nearest and keep the grouping of each product: the acceleration
///   factor is squared before it meets the value, and the angular factor is
///   multiplied by pi / 180 first. Results are stored as 32-bit floats.

/// \file INIParseAudioLODDefinition.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_ini_units_name_keys
/// \brief The AudioLOD block parser.
///
/// \see INI::parseAudioLODDefinition

