/// \fn static void INI::parseDurationReal(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: reads a duration in milliseconds and stores it as a
///        real number of logic frames.
///
/// Used for time fields stored as a Real rather than as a whole number of
/// frames. Fields read with it include TimeForFullHeal,
/// BoredTime, WorkerRespawnDelay, DPSRampupTime, RadarExtendTime and
/// SuperweaponCountdownFlashDuration (from the retail FieldParse rows that
/// point at it).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads the next token with INI::scanReal and stores the value times the
/// duration factor (frames per millisecond; see
/// \ref grp_w1019_ini_units_name_keys). Unlike
/// INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt and INI::parseDurationUnsignedShort, it
/// does not round: at the initial factor, 300 ms becomes 1.5 frames. The
/// object being filled and the row's userData are not used. Zero Hour's
/// version is the same with the constant LOGICFRAMES_PER_MSEC_REAL.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Unused.
/// \param store    [out] The Real field to write.
/// \param userData Unused.
///
/// \see INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, INI::parseDurationUnsignedShort,
///      INI::parseVelocityReal

/// \fn static void INI::parseVelocityReal(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: reads a speed in distance per second and stores it
///        in distance per logic frame.
///
/// The generic parser for speeds and other per-second rates. Fields read
/// with it include Speed, MinWeaponSpeed, MaxWeaponSpeed, WeaponSpeed,
/// SinkRate, ShockWaveSpeed, CrushKnockback, RamPower, MaximumAudioSpeed and
/// TreadAnimationRate (from the retail FieldParse rows that point at it).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads the next token with INI::scanReal and stores the shared
/// seconds-per-logic-frame factor times the value. The object being filled
/// and the row's userData are not used. Zero Hour's version computes the
/// same product with the constant SECONDS_PER_LOGICFRAME_REAL
/// (ConvertVelocityInSecsToFrames in GameCommon.h).
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Unused.
/// \param store    [out] The Real field to write.
/// \param userData Unused.
///
/// \see INI::parseAccelerationReal, INI::parseAngularVelocityReal,
///      INI::parseDurationReal

/// \fn static void INI::parseAccelerationReal(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: reads an acceleration in distance per second squared
///        and stores it in distance per logic frame squared.
///
/// Gravity is one field read with it (from the retail FieldParse rows that
/// point at it).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads the next token with INI::scanReal and stores the square of the
/// shared seconds-per-logic-frame factor times the value. The square is
/// computed on every call rather than folded into a constant, because the
/// factor is a writable global. The object being filled and the row's
/// userData are not used. Zero Hour: the same grouping with constants
/// (ConvertAccelerationInSecsToFrames in GameCommon.h).
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Unused.
/// \param store    [out] The Real field to write.
/// \param userData Unused.
///
/// \see INI::parseVelocityReal

/// \fn static void INI::parseAngularVelocityReal(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: reads a turn rate in degrees per second and stores it
///        in radians per logic frame.
///
/// Fields read with it include TurretTurnRate, TurretPitchRate,
/// ExitPitchRate and BounceAmount (from the retail FieldParse rows that point
/// at it).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads the next token with INI::scanReal, multiplies the shared
/// seconds-per-logic-frame factor by pi / 180 and stores the value times that
/// product. The object being filled and the row's userData are not used.
/// Zero Hour's version (ConvertAngularVelocityInDegreesPerSecToRadsPerFrame
/// in GameCommon.h) has the same grouping with a constant factor.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Unused.
/// \param store    [out] The Real field to write.
/// \param userData Unused.
///
/// \see INI::parseVelocityReal

/// \fn static void INI::parseAudioLODDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \brief Block parser for AudioLOD: fills the audio settings of one audio
///        detail level in TheGameLODManager.
///
/// The game has two audio detail levels, named Low and High. Each AudioLOD
/// block names one level and sets its fields:
///
///     AudioLOD Low
///       MaximumAmbientStreams = 4
///       AllowDolby = No
///       AllowReverb = No
///     End
///
/// (the values are illustrative). MaximumAmbientStreams is read with
/// INI::parseInt, AllowDolby and AllowReverb with INI::parseBool; the field
/// names, parsers and level names were read from the retail executable's
/// tables. The player's choice is kept as a level name in the options
/// preferences under the key AudioLOD (OptionPreferences::getAudioLOD turns
/// it back into an index), and the audio manager consults the current level
/// when it chooses a 3D provider (MilesAudioManager::onAudioLODChanged;
/// \ref sub_audio).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Reads the level name and, when TheGameLODManager exists, turns it into
///   an index with GameLODManager::getAudioLODIndex, which compares it with
///   the two names without regard to case.
/// - An unknown name throws an INIException with the message "Unknown Audio
///   LOD level '%s'", which stops the load of the file.
/// - Otherwise it fills that level's settings with INI::initFromINI and the
///   three-field table. The settings are filled in place, not reset first, so
///   a later block for the same level changes only the fields it names.
/// - When TheGameLODManager does not exist it reads the name and does
///   nothing else.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the AudioLOD keyword.
///
/// \note Zero Hour has no AudioLOD block. BFME 1 has the same parser, the
///       same three fields and the same two levels.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The two level names and the three fields are compiled in: data can tune
/// each level but cannot add a level or a field. Level names in data are
/// matched without regard to case; field names, as everywhere, exactly.
///
/// \see GameLODManager::getAudioLODIndex, OptionPreferences::getAudioLOD,
///      MilesAudioManager::onAudioLODChanged

/// \class SubsystemInterfaceList
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_ini_units_name_keys
/// \brief The engine's list of registered subsystems, in registration order,
///        and the passes that run over all of them.
///
/// GameEngine::init registers each service, such as TheWeaponStore and
/// TheAudio, in this list (\ref sub_engine_core_registration describes how,
/// including SubsystemInterfaceList::initSubsystem and the legend load).
/// The list then drives whole-engine passes:
/// SubsystemInterfaceList::postProcessLoadAll once start-up data is in, and
/// SubsystemInterfaceList::resetAll, which walks the list in reverse and is
/// called by GameEngine::reset and GameEngine::init.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// In BFME 2 each entry pairs the subsystem with a small slot object that
/// owns it and clears its global when destroyed (in Zero Hour the list holds
/// plain pointers). \ref sub_engine_core_classes describes the singleton,
/// TheSubsystemList, and how the engine's core classes fit together.

/// \fn void SubsystemInterfaceList::postProcessLoadAll()
/// \brief Calls every registered subsystem's post-load step, in registration
///        order.
///
/// The hook for work that needs all start-up data, for example resolving
/// names that refer to definitions another subsystem loads. Its only caller
/// is GameEngine::init, near the end of start-up; \ref page_startup_init
/// places it in the start-up sequence. Since GameEngine::init is the only
/// caller, the pass does not run again for later loads such as a map's own
/// INI files.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Walks the entries from first to last and calls each subsystem's
/// post-load virtual (Zero Hour: SubsystemInterface::postProcessLoad, empty
/// by default). Zero Hour's list walks its plain pointers the same way.
///
/// \see SubsystemInterfaceList::resetAll, SubsystemInterfaceList::initSubsystem

/// \fn unsigned int calcHashForString(const char *p)
/// \brief Hashes a name, case-sensitively, to choose its bucket in the
///        name-key table.
///
/// NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey takes this value modulo the bucket count
/// (45007, as in Zero Hour) and searches that bucket.
/// \ref sub_common_services_namekeys describes how names become keys.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For each byte, the hash so far times 33 plus the byte, starting from 0:
/// the DJB2 recurrence without its usual seed of 5381, in 32-bit unsigned
/// arithmetic that wraps. Bytes are read as signed chars, so a byte above
/// 127 adds a negative value. Zero Hour's source has the same function
/// inline in NameKeyGenerator.cpp; in BFME 2 it is a separate function.
///
/// \param p The name, NUL-terminated; must not be null.
/// \return The hash.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Within NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey the bucket only narrows the search:
/// names in it are compared exactly, and a new key is taken from the
/// generator's counter only when the name is not found. Whether other code
/// adds names to the same table with a different hash, which would let the
/// choice of hash change which keys are issued and their numbering, is not
/// established; keep the hash as it is to reproduce retail key assignment.
///
/// \see calcHashForLowercaseString, NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey

/// \fn unsigned int calcHashForLowercaseString(const char *p)
/// \brief Hashes a name, ignoring case, to choose its bucket for
///        NameKeyGenerator::nameToLowercaseKey.
///
/// The case-insensitive twin of calcHashForString: nameToLowercaseKey takes
/// this value modulo the bucket count and compares names in that bucket
/// without regard to case.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same recurrence, applied to each byte after the C runtime's tolower.
/// Bytes are read as unsigned (0 to 255) before tolower. Zero Hour's source
/// passes the bytes to tolower as signed chars, so BFME 2 and Zero Hour can
/// give different values for names with bytes above 127.
/// calcHashForString adds bytes as signed values and does not fold case, so
/// the two BFME 2 hashes generally give different values for names with
/// capital letters or bytes above 127; neither can stand in for the other.
///
/// \param p The name, NUL-terminated; must not be null.
/// \return The hash.
///
/// \see calcHashForString, NameKeyGenerator::nameToLowercaseKey

/// \class WeaponStore
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_ini_units_name_keys
/// \brief The registry of weapon definitions (TheWeaponStore): owns every
///        WeaponTemplate and creates the Weapon objects that use them.
///
/// WeaponStore is a registered subsystem. WeaponStore::parseWeaponTemplateDefinition
/// reads each Weapon INI block into a WeaponTemplate, which other definitions
/// then refer to by name (WeaponStore::findWeaponTemplate; the reference
/// parser INI::parseWeaponTemplate resolves names while fields are read).
/// At run time WeaponStore::allocateNewWeapon gives objects, weapon sets and
/// modules their own Weapon instances, and WeaponStore::createAndFireTempWeapon
/// fires a weapon that no object holds.
/// The store is updated with the other logic stores during the logic frame
/// (\ref page_frame_loop_logic). \ref page_core_frameworks_weapons gives the
/// overview of weapons, damage and armor.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// The store owns the templates. A Weapon it allocates belongs to the
/// caller. A definition loaded again with the override load type (Zero Hour
/// uses that type for a map's own INI) becomes an override template that
/// takes the original's place and keeps a link to it; WeaponStore::reset
/// deletes such overrides and puts the originals back. The reload load type keeps the old template
/// aside instead (\ref page_core_frameworks_reload).
///
/// \par Modding
/// Weapon definitions are INI data and simulation inputs: every peer must
/// load the same ones (\ref page_core_frameworks_modding).

/// \fn Weapon *WeaponStore::allocateNewWeapon(const WeaponTemplate *tmpl, WeaponSlotType slot) const
/// \brief Creates a new Weapon for a weapon template and slot; the caller
///        owns it.
///
/// Code that makes a new weapon from a weapon template goes through this
/// function (Implementation below gives the evidence). Callers include
/// Object::initObject, WeaponSet::updateWeaponSet, and WeaponSet::xfer and
/// FireWeaponUpdate::xfer (which rebuild weapons when a game is loaded),
/// the constructors of FireWeaponWhenDamagedBehavior,
/// WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate and FireWeaponCollide, and the store's own
/// WeaponStore::createAndFireTempWeapon, which makes a temporary weapon to
/// fire once. BFME 2 also contains a Weapon copy constructor, which this
/// function does not use; what calls it is not established.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Allocates a Weapon and runs its constructor with the template and slot.
/// Zero Hour: Weapon's (template, slot) constructor is protected and
/// WeaponStore is a friend, so other code must create weapons through this
/// function. In BFME 2 the call graph shows this function as the
/// constructor's only direct caller. Zero Hour defines this
/// function inline in Weapon.h and takes the memory from the Weapon memory
/// pool; BFME 2 has it as an out-of-line function that allocates with the
/// global operator new (\ref sub_memory describes where that memory comes
/// from).
///
/// \param tmpl The weapon definition the new weapon uses.
/// \param slot The weapon slot the weapon occupies (primary, secondary and
///             so on).
/// \return The new Weapon, owned by the caller.
///
/// \see WeaponStore::findWeaponTemplate, WeaponStore::createAndFireTempWeapon

/// \class CommandSet
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_ini_units_name_keys
/// \brief A named set of command button slots, defined by a CommandSet INI
///        block, that an object's UI and orders draw on.
///
/// ControlBar::parseCommandSetDefinition reads each CommandSet block; the
/// sets belong to TheControlBar, and object definitions refer to a set by
/// name. Each slot holds a CommandButton defined elsewhere (Zero Hour names
/// the slots 1 to 18 in the block; BFME 2's sets hold 32). Code that reads a
/// slot normally goes through CommandSet::getCommandButton, which applies the
/// game logic's overrides. \ref page_core_frameworks_commands explains how buttons connect
/// the interface to logic commands.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// Sets live as long as TheControlBar holds them. A second block with the
/// name of an existing set is an error in a normal load ("Duplicate
/// commandset %s found!"). The override load type creates an override
/// instead, and the reload load type replaces the set
/// (\ref page_core_frameworks_reload).
///
/// \par Modding
/// Command sets are INI data that the logic reads as well as the UI (see
/// CommandSet::getCommandButton), so all peers need the same definitions.

/// \fn const CommandButton *CommandSet::getCommandButton(Int i) const
/// \brief Returns the button in slot i, or the button the game logic has put
///        in its place.
///
/// The accessor for a command set's slots, used by both halves of the game.
/// Logic-side callers include GameLogic::logicMessageDispatcher,
/// AIUpdateInterface::privateCommandButton, BuildAssistant::isPossibleToMakeUnit,
/// AIPlayer::buildUpgrade and map-script actions and conditions;
/// client-side callers include ControlBar::addCommonCommands and the Apt
/// spell book (AptInGameSpellBookInterface).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// When TheGameLogic exists and has an override recorded for this set's name
/// and slot i, returns the overriding button, which may be null (an empty
/// slot). Otherwise returns the button the INI definition put in slot i. The
/// null check on TheGameLogic is there, by Zero Hour's comment, for
/// WorldBuilder, which reads command sets without a game logic. Overrides
/// are recorded by GameLogic::setControlBarOverride; the map-script action
/// ScriptActions::doRemoveCommandBarButton records a null one to remove a
/// button, and a second script action records a button (its Zero Hour and
/// BFME 1 counterpart is ScriptActions::doAddCommandBarButton).
///
/// \param i The slot index, from 0. It is not checked against the number of
///          slots.
/// \return The button for the slot, or null when the slot is empty or an
///         override cleared it.
///
/// \note Zero Hour has the same override lookup in this function
///       (GameLogic::findControlBarOverride); it is not new in BFME 2. Zero
///       Hour keys its override map by a string whose first character is '0'
///       plus the slot number, followed by the set name; BFME 2 keys it by
///       the slot number and the set name together.
///
/// \par Modding
/// An override is keyed by set name, so it applies to every object type that
/// uses that set, not only the one a script names. Because the override map
/// is game logic state and logic code reads the slots, script changes to
/// command bars are part of the simulation.
///
/// \see GameLogic::setControlBarOverride, ScriptActions::doRemoveCommandBarButton,
///      ControlBar::parseCommandSetDefinition
